{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's distinction between 'critical preference' and 'inductive inference' fails to resolve the problem's core challenge regarding action. While the target argues that preferring a theory is merely a methodological rule for error avoidance, it does not explain why this rule is rational for *future* action without assuming that past success (surviving tests) correlates with future reliability. If the agent acts on the better-corroborated theory because they expect it to work better than untested alternatives in the future, they are implicitly relying on the uniformity of nature—the very inductive principle Popper rejected. The target asserts the preference is 'defensible' but offers no non-inductive justification for why minimizing 'known error' via past tests should guide decisions about unknown future outcomes, thus smuggling induction back in under the guise of 'critical rationality'.","premise":"Acting on a theory requires an expectation that its past performance is relevant to its future success.","premises_essential":["108c1f5a3330cb52997a4bcffbee9347256500f59deb0fb17984364336d24412"],"successor_question":"Can a Popperian provide a justification for acting on the best-corroborated theory that does not implicitly assume the future will resemble the past?","target":"108c1f5a3330cb52997a4bcffbee9347256500f59deb0fb17984364336d24412"}]}